      "text": "Page 1\r\nB2B, B2C & C2C\r\nDetailed Explanation, Examples, Applications & Differences\r\n1. Introduction\r\nB2B, B2C and C2C are common business and e-commerce models. They describe the relationship between\r\nthe seller and the buyer. B2B means Business-to-Business, B2C means Business-to-Consumer, and C2C\r\nmeans Consumer-to-Consumer.\r\n2. B2B \u2013 Business to Business\r\nMeaning: B2B is a business model in which one business sells products or services to another business. The\r\nbuyer may be a company, manufacturer, retailer, institution or other organization.\r\nSimple Example: Intel \u2192 Laptop Manufacturer \u2192 Customer\r\nApplications:\r\n\u2022 Wholesale: manufacturers sell products in bulk to wholesalers or retailers.\r\n\u2022 Manufacturing: companies buy raw materials, components and machinery from suppliers.\r\n\u2022 Software and SaaS: businesses purchase CRM, accounting, cybersecurity and productivity software.\r\n\u2022 Cloud computing: organizations purchase computing, storage and infrastructure services.\r\n\u2022 Banking and finance: banks provide corporate loans, payment processing and business accounts.\r\n\u2022 Supply-chain management: businesses purchase inventory, logistics and procurement services.\r\nKey Characteristics:\r\n\u2022 Customers are businesses or organizations.\r\n\u2022 Orders and transaction values are generally larger.\r\n\u2022 Sales cycles are usually longer and may involve negotiation.\r\n\u2022 Contracts, customized pricing and long-term relationships are common.\r\n\u2022 Buying decisions focus strongly on cost, efficiency, ROI and business requirements.\r\n3. B2C \u2013 Business to Consumer\r\nMeaning: B2C is a business model in which a business sells products or services directly to individual\r\nconsumers for personal use. It is the model most commonly experienced in retail and online shopping.\r\nSimple Example: Business \u2192 Consumer \u2192 Personal Use\r\nApplications:\r\n\u2022 Online shopping: consumers buy clothing, electronics, books, groceries and other products.\r\n\u2022 Food delivery: restaurants and food platforms serve individual customers.\r\n\u2022 Entertainment: consumers purchase streaming, music or gaming subscriptions.\r\n\u2022 Travel and tourism: individuals book flights, hotels and holiday packages.\r\n\u2022 Banking: banks provide savings accounts, credit cards and personal loans.",
