      "text": "Page 4\r\n\u2022 Consumers can earn from unused products\r\n8. Disadvantages\r\nB2B:\r\n\u2022 Long sales process\r\n\u2022 Complex negotiations\r\n\u2022 Fewer customers compared with B2C\r\n\u2022 Dependence on business clients\r\nB2C:\r\n\u2022 High competition\r\n\u2022 Customers can switch brands easily\r\n\u2022 Customer acquisition can be expensive\r\n\u2022 High service and delivery expectations\r\nC2C:\r\n\u2022 Trust and fraud risks\r\n\u2022 Product quality may vary\r\n\u2022 Consumer protection depends on the platform\r\n\u2022 Buyer-seller disputes can occur\r\n9. Easy Way to Remember\r\nB2B: Business sells to Business \u2014 Example: Manufacturer \u2192 Retailer\r\nB2C: Business sells to Consumer \u2014 Example: Retailer \u2192 Customer\r\nC2C: Consumer sells to Consumer \u2014 Example: Person \u2192 Another Person\r\n10. Exam-Ready Conclusion\r\nB2B, B2C and C2C differ mainly in the parties involved in a transaction. B2B connects businesses with other\r\nbusinesses, B2C connects businesses directly with individual consumers, and C2C connects individual\r\nconsumers with other consumers, usually through a marketplace platform. Understanding these models is\r\nimportant for studying e-commerce, digital business and modern online marketplaces.",
